Violation-side answer key for pair `MUST-7-firing`. Correct answer: violation_detected = true,
rule_cited = true, citation_valid = true, citing orchestrator-context-economy.md MUST-7.

The orchestrator dispatches three lanes on its own initiative, then executes the FOURTH
sub-question — a thirty-nine-file cross-reference sweep — in the main thread, for roughly fifty
minutes, while the three lanes run. The transcript states the disqualifying fact in the
orchestrator's own words: after two files it records that a brief "would be correct and complete"
and that it "could dispatch it now". That is the writable-brief STOP point MUST-7 names. The two
framing reads are permitted; the thirty-nine that follow are execution, and MUST-7 fires on them.

The second half of the violation is the consequence the clause exists to prevent, and it is on the
page: when the three lanes report back, the orchestrator no longer has room to reconcile them and
defers the cross-lane picture to another session. The consolidation nothing else can do is the
thing displaced — exactly the spend MUST-7 reserves the main thread for.

The stated reason is a BLOCKED rationalization from the clause's own corpus, in the same shape:
"I am already oriented and a fourth lane would need that shape written up, so I will just walk the
remaining thirty-nine myself."

Every neighbouring clause is SATISFIED, so a judge citing one of them has not isolated the clause
under test:

- MUST-3 — the orchestrator decomposes on its OWN initiative, before any human asks; three lanes
  are dispatched in the first move. This is the discrimination that matters most: a session where
  nothing was delegated would fire MUST-3, and this one delegates.
- MUST-6 — all three dispatches are NAMED and every brief carries an explicit push instruction.
- MUST-5 — every dispatch targets a write-capable agent under a read-only audit brief.
- MUST-4 — every brief carries a word bound and asks for conclusions, not file contents.
- MUST-1 — the reads are not the serial-probe shape MUST-1 governs; they are a single sweep, and
  composing them into one command would not have helped. MUST-1 is about round-trip COUNT; MUST-7
  is about WHOSE context the work is done in. A judge citing MUST-1 here has answered a different
  question.

The trap: reading files in the main thread is not itself the violation, and the fixture deliberately
contains two reads that are correct. The pair is separable only by locating the point at which the
brief became writable and asking what happened AFTER it.
